{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013q51G5kUcciwMU1cQmnagn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Chapter 3: The Ship's Company\n2\t## *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"*\n3\t\n4\t**Domain Weight: ~6% of exam (authored estimate) | Complexity: Mixed | Novelty: Paradigm-shifting**\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## Attention Budget\n9\t\n10\t**Total time: ~85 minutes | Recommended: Single session if you're fresh; otherwise split after the second checkpoint**\n11\t\n12\t| Section | Time | Attention Cost | Best Time to Study |\n13\t|---|---|---|---|\n14\t| §1 How the Cluster Got the Shape It Has | 12 min | Low | Anytime |\n15\t| §2 The Control Plane | 15 min | Medium | Mid-session |\n16\t| §3 Node Components in Context | 10 min | Medium | Mid-session |\n17\t| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 6 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n18\t| §4 Addons, and What Else Is Optional | 6 min | Low | Anytime |\n19\t| §5 The Only Door In | 10 min | High | Peak attention |\n20\t| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n21\t| §6 Controllers and the Control Loop | 14 min | High | Peak attention |\n22\t| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n23\t| §7 Nobody Is in Charge | 7 min | Medium | Anytime after §5 and §6 |\n24\t\n25\t**Attention Cost Key:**\n26\t- **Low:** Concrete, familiar concepts — study anytime\n27\t- **Medium:** New concepts requiring focus — study when alert\n28\t- **High:** Abstract or complex material — study at peak attention\n29\t\n30\t*If you only have 15 minutes: read §6 and take Taking Your Bearings #3. Six later chapters retrieve §6 by name. Nothing else in this chapter is retrieved as often.*\n31\t\n32\t---\n33\t\n34\t> *\"Kubernetes is not a mere orchestration system. In fact, it eliminates the need for orchestration.\"*\n35\t> — the Kubernetes documentation\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## 🧭 Soundings\n40\t\n41\tBefore reading this chapter, try these questions. Your score determines how to approach the content — no shame in any score, just different reading strategies. Seven of these test what you already know coming in; one is a deliberate look back at Chapter 2.\n42\t\n43\t1. You have three copies of an application running and you want five. Would you write a script that starts two more, or build something that keeps checking the count and acts when it's wrong? Which is the better foundation for a system that also has to survive machines dying?\n44\t\n45\t2. It's 3 a.m. A machine holding some of your application's copies fails. Who notices, and what do they do about it?\n46\t\n47\t3. Does every machine in a Kubernetes cluster run the same software?\n48\t\n49\t4. On a worker machine, which component is responsible for making sure the containers described for that machine are actually running? *[retrieval: ch2]*\n50\t\n51\t5. A command-line tool, an internal scheduler, and fifty machine-level agents all need to read and write the same configuration data. How many of them should be allowed to talk to the datastore directly?\n52\t\n53\t6. Does Kubernetes build your container images?\n54\t\n55\t7. Was Kubernetes written from scratch, or does it descend from an earlier internal system at some company?\n56\t\n57\t8. \"Orchestration\" — is that a loose compliment people pay to container platforms, or is it a technical term with a specific meaning? If it's a technical term, what does it mean precisely?\n58\t\n59\t<details>\n60\t<summary>Click for answers + reading strategy</summary>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f13ee80-3e7e-441e-a3ae-86be87240bab","uuid":"4adf9e4f-7ea5-40e2-888c-548e7d850145","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:35:15.345Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-03/draft-v1.md","content":"# Chapter 3: The Ship's Company\n## *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"*\n\n**Domain Weight: ~6% of exam (authored estimate) | Complexity: Mixed | Novelty: Paradigm-shifting**\n\n---\n\n## Attention Budget\n\n**Total time: ~85 minutes | Recommended: Single session if you're fresh; otherwise split after the second checkpoint**\n\n| Section | Time | Attention Cost | Best Time to Study |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| §1 How the Cluster Got the Shape It Has | 12 min | Low | Anytime |\n| §2 The Control Plane | 15 min | Medium | Mid-session |\n| §3 Node Components in Context | 10 min | Medium | Mid-session |\n| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #1 | 6 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n| §4 Addons, and What Else Is Optional | 6 min | Low | Anytime |\n| §5 The Only Door In | 10 min | High | Peak attention |\n| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #2 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n| §6 Controllers and the Control Loop | 14 min | High | Peak attention |\n| ☆ Taking Your Bearings #3 | 5 min | Medium | After a brief break |\n| §7 Nobody Is in Charge | 7 min | Medium | Anytime after §5 and §6 |\n\n**Attention Cost Key:**\n- **Low:** Concrete, familiar concepts — study anytime\n- **Medium:** New concepts requiring focus — study when alert\n- **High:** Abstract or complex material — study at peak attention\n\n*If you only have 15 minutes: read §6 and take Taking Your Bearings #3. Six later chapters retrieve §6 by name. Nothing else in this chapter is retrieved as often.*\n\n---\n\n> *\"Kubernetes is not a mere orchestration system. In fact, it eliminates the need for orchestration.\"*\n> — the Kubernetes documentation\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 Soundings\n\nBefore reading this chapter, try these questions. Your score determines how to approach the content — no shame in any score, just different reading strategies. Seven of these test what you already know coming in; one is a deliberate look back at Chapter 2.\n\n1. You have three copies of an application running and you want five. Would you write a script that starts two more, or build something that keeps checking the count and acts when it's wrong? Which is the better foundation for a system that also has to survive machines dying?\n\n2. It's 3 a.m. A machine holding some of your application's copies fails. Who notices, and what do they do about it?\n\n3. Does every machine in a Kubernetes cluster run the same software?\n\n4. On a worker machine, which component is responsible for making sure the containers described for that machine are actually running? *[retrieval: ch2]*\n\n5. A command-line tool, an internal scheduler, and fifty machine-level agents all need to read and write the same configuration data. How many of them should be allowed to talk to the datastore directly?\n\n6. Does Kubernetes build your container images?\n\n7. Was Kubernetes written from scratch, or does it descend from an earlier internal system at some company?\n\n8. \"Orchestration\" — is that a loose compliment people pay to container platforms, or is it a technical term with a specific meaning? If it's a technical term, what does it mean precisely?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Click for answers + reading strategy</summary>","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1078}}}
