{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UXvajctEbrBnBz9Y6vP1hN","type":"tool_result","content":"18:| §4 Addons, and What Else Is Optional | 6 min | Low | Anytime |\n78:**If you got 6+ right:** Skim, with two exceptions. Read §6 at full pace regardless of your score, because six later chapters retrieve it by name. And read §4 rather than skimming it; the optionality material scores well on intuition and still catches people on exam day.\n105:- **Name** every control-plane and node component, state its one job, and mark which two are optional — and under what circumstances.\n177:[Omitted long matching line]\n227:│   │ kube-controller-manager │   ╎ cloud-controller-manager╎  │\n234:│  │ kubelet │  ╎ kube-proxy ╎     │  │  │ kubelet │  ╎ kube-  ...\n242:  ╭╌╌╌╮ dashed border = OPTIONAL (see §4)\n281:### cloud-controller-manager\n285:That last sentence is the one to hold onto. This component is genuinely optional. Not \"optional but everyone runs it\" — *absent* from an entire class of real clusters, including the one on your laptop. §4 comes back to it.\n287:[Omitted long matching line]\n309:### kube-proxy (optional)\n311:[Omitted long matching line]\n313:Note the precise hedge: it implements *part* of the Service concept. Service is a Kubernetes object with its own chapter; kube-proxy is the node-level machinery that makes some of it work. And note the optionality condition, because \"optional\" here has a specific trigger — something else doing the same job — not a general \"you can skip it if you like.\"\n315:*[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 — Services, and how kube-proxy implements them]*\n327:> **Control plane:** kube-apiserver, etcd, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, and cloud-controller-manager *(optional — absent on premises and on your laptop)*.\n329:> **Node** (on every node): kubelet, kube-proxy *(optional — unnecessary if a network plugin provides equivalent packet forwarding)*, and the container runtime.\n331:> Eight names. Two of them optional, for two different reasons. One of them — the container runtime — is not Kubernetes software. [source: k8s-docs-components-2026-08-23] [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]\n333:[Omitted long matching line]\n344:B) kube-proxy\n346:D) cloud-controller-manager\n371:A) kube-proxy, via iptables rules\n380:**Q1 — B.** kube-proxy is a node component; it runs on each node in the cluster, maintaining network rules [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23].\n383:- **D is wrong** — cloud-controller-manager is a control plane component, and an optional one at that.\n400:- **A is wrong** — kube-proxy handles network rules for Services. It has nothing to do with starting containers.\n411:✓ Which two are optional, and the fact that the reasons differ\n418:## §4 — 🔵 Addons, and What Else Is Optional\n428:### Three kinds of optional\n430:You have now met eight components and four addons, and three items in that set of twelve are marked optional in the documentation, for three genuinely different reasons:\n432:| What | Why it's optional |\n434:| **kube-proxy** | Because something else can do its job. If your network plugin implements equivalent packet forwarding for Services, kube-proxy is redundant [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. |\n435:| **cloud-controller-manager** | Because there may be no cloud to talk to. On premises or on your laptop, the cluster simply does not have one [source: k8s-docs-cluster-architecture-2026-08-23]. |\n440:> **\"kube-proxy runs on every node, always.\"** The documentation says node components run on every node, then marks kube-proxy optional in the same breath. Both are true: *when it runs, it runs on every node*, but it may not run at all, if a network plugin does the same work.\n442:> **\"Every cluster has a cloud-controller-manager.\"** No. Every cluster on a cloud provider that integrates one does. On premises, or on a laptop, that component is simply absent.\n444:> The shared lesson is duller and more valuable than either fact: **read the word \"optional\" when the documentation prints it.** It's printed rarely and it's printed deliberately.\n523:## ☆ Taking Your Bearings: Arrangement and Optionality\n527:**Q1.** 🔵 Under what condition is kube-proxy unnecessary on your nodes?\n532:D) kube-proxy is never unnecessary; it's a required node component\n534:**Q2.** 🔵 You have three clusters: one on a managed cloud provider, one in your company's own data center, and one running on your laptop for learning. In which of them does a cloud-controller-manager run?\n539:D) None — cloud-controller-manager is not a real component\n552:C) A message routed by kube-proxy from the control plane to the node\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 40]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f13ee80-3e7e-441e-a3ae-86be87240bab","uuid":"96015cfe-9d24-466b-8db5-5d45351314e9","timestamp":"2026-08-24T07:35:47.524Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"18:| §4 Addons, and What Else Is Optional | 6 min | Low | Anytime |\n78:**If you got 6+ right:** Skim, with two exceptions. Read §6 at full pace regardless of your score, because six later chapters retrieve it by name. And read §4 rather than skimming it; the optionality material scores well on intuition and still catches people on exam day.\n105:- **Name** every control-plane and node component, state its one job, and mark which two are optional — and under what circumstances.\n177:[Omitted long matching line]\n227:│   │ kube-controller-manager │   ╎ cloud-controller-manager╎  │\n234:│  │ kubelet │  ╎ kube-proxy ╎     │  │  │ kubelet │  ╎ kube-  ...\n242:  ╭╌╌╌╮ dashed border = OPTIONAL (see §4)\n281:### cloud-controller-manager\n285:That last sentence is the one to hold onto. This component is genuinely optional. Not \"optional but everyone runs it\" — *absent* from an entire class of real clusters, including the one on your laptop. §4 comes back to it.\n287:[Omitted long m